@page "/projects/homelab"

<ProjectLayout Project="@ProjectData.Get("homelab")">
    <div>
        <h2>What?</h2>
        <p>I run my own homelab using Proxmox on a small Fujitsu ESPRIMO mini pc.</p>
        <p>I use virtual machines running Docker to host my own services, websites, and a Minecraft server. This includes things like Nextcloud for file storage, Jellyfin for media streaming, Vaultwarden for passwords and more.</p>
    </div>

    <div>
        <h2>Why?</h2>
        <p>I care a lot about privacy, so I try to replace as many big-corp services as I can.</p>
        <p>It's usefull for running projects like <a href="https://github.com/alexjonker/portfolio">this portfolio site</a>.</p>
        <p>And I enjoy tinkering around with linux in virtual machines.</p>
    </div>

    <div>
        <h2>How?</h2>
        <p>I use Proxmox to manage my virtual machines. Most of my applications run in Docker containers, which makes them easier to manage and update.</p>
        <p>To make my projects accessible from outside my home network, I rent a VPS from Hetzner in Germany and run <a href="https://github.com/fosrl/pangolin">Pangolin</a> as a reverse proxy.</p>
        <p>This lets me expose my websites and services to the internet without exposing my home IP address.</p>
        <p>I manage my Docker containers using <a href="https://github.com/getarcaneapp/arcane" >Arcane</a> and host my own projects like <a href="https://github.com/alexjonker/portfolio">this portfolio site</a>, <a href="/projects/tidawnloader">Tidawnloader</a> and many other services like the ones mentioned before.</p>
    </div>

    <div>
        <h2>Gallery:</h2>
        <div class="gallery">
            <img src="@Assets["content/homelab/mini-pc.jpg"]" alt="Mini pc"/>
        </div>
    </div>
</ProjectLayout>